        "type": "markdown",
        "content": "## 1. The 07:15 AM Stamping Press Amputation Near-Miss\n\nAt 07:15 on a Tuesday morning inside a tier-1 automotive chassis stamping facility, the plant safety siren echoed across the press bay. A 150-ton hydraulic C-frame stamping press had just cycled while an operator was reaching into the die bed to dislodge a jammed sheet metal blank.\n\nThe operator survived with severe crushing injuries, escaping traumatic amputation by fractions of an inch. During the subsequent OSHA investigation and root-cause analysis, investigators discovered a wooden wedge wrapped in electrical tape forced into the left-hand actuation button (`PB-101A`).\n\nThe machine builder had written naive boolean concurrence inside a standard programmable controller:\n\n```\nIF Left_PB AND Right_PB THEN Clutch_Solenoid = TRUE\nELSE Clutch_Solenoid = FALSE\n```\n\nTo boost their piece-rate production quota, the operator had wedged the left button permanently down, freeing their left hand to load sheet metal blanks while cycling the 150-ton ram single-handedly using only the right button! While reaching into the pinch zone to clear an off-center slug, their hip accidentally brushed against the right button. The hydraulic ram surged downward with 3,000 PSI of fluid pressure.\n\nThe incident resulted in a $240,000 regulatory fine, immediate revocation of machine CE certification, and an emergency plant-wide safety shutdown across all twelve stamping lines."
