        "content": "## 4. Stage 2: The 500 ms Synchronism Window & Anti-Tie-Down\n\nTo eliminate the tie-down bypass, ISO 13851 mandates **Synchronous Actuation**:\nBoth pushbuttons must experience a transition from the unactuated state to the actuated state within a time window of **500 milliseconds** ($t_s \\le 0.5\\text{ s}$).\n\nIn our Stage 2 circuit, we implement synchronous timing using Rising Edge Triggers (`R_TRIG`) and Off-Delay Timers (`TOF`, 500 ms):\n1. When `PB-101A` is pressed, `edge1` fires a single-scan pulse into `window1` (`TOF(500)`), opening a 500 ms acceptance window.\n2. If `PB-101B` is pressed while `window1` is active, `sync1` turns true.\n3. Conversely, if `PB-101B` is pressed first, `window2` opens and accepts `PB-101A` within 500 ms.\n4. If either button is held permanently, its `R_TRIG` edge only fires once. On the next cycle, no rising edge occurs, the synchronism window never opens, and the press refuses to stroke!"
